Q1. What is a Breakpoint?

Interview Ready Answer

A breakpoint is a debugging marker placed on a specific line of code that pauses program execution when that line is reached. It allows developers to inspect variable values, analyze the call stack, examine the execution flow, and identify the root cause of bugs before continuing execution.

Definition

A breakpoint is a debugging marker that temporarily pauses program execution.

Purpose

• Pause execution at a specific line.
• Inspect variable values.
• Analyze the execution flow.
• Check the call stack.
• Identify and fix bugs efficiently.

======================================================================

Q2. Why are breakpoints used?

Interview Ready Answer

Breakpoints are used to pause program execution at a desired location so that developers can inspect the application's current state, analyze variable values, trace the execution flow, and identify the root cause of defects.

Purpose

• Debug complex logic.
• Verify variable values.
• Analyze function execution.
• Detect the root cause of bugs.
• Reduce dependency on console.log() statements.

======================================================================

Q3. What are the different types of breakpoints available in modern debuggers?

Interview Ready Answer

Modern debuggers provide multiple types of breakpoints:

• Line Breakpoint
• Conditional Breakpoint
• Exception Breakpoint
• Function Breakpoint
• Logpoint

Definition

Line Breakpoint
• Pauses execution whenever the specified line is reached.

Conditional Breakpoint
• Pauses execution only when a specified condition evaluates to true.

Exception Breakpoint
• Pauses execution when an exception is thrown.

Function Breakpoint
• Pauses execution whenever a specified function is called.

Logpoint
• Prints a log message without pausing program execution.

======================================================================

Q4. What is a Conditional Breakpoint?

Interview Ready Answer

A conditional breakpoint is a breakpoint that pauses program execution only when a specified condition evaluates to true. It is useful when a section of code executes repeatedly, but the developer wants to stop execution only for a specific scenario.

Definition

A conditional breakpoint pauses execution only if a given condition is satisfied.

Purpose

• Debug loops efficiently.
• Stop only for specific data.
• Reduce unnecessary pauses.
• Analyze a particular execution scenario.

Example Use Cases

• Stop when count == 100
• Stop when userId == 101
• Stop when price > 5000

